nodecode-rex is a rewrite of kshitijk4poor's kshitijk4poor/hermes-rex-plugin:

  https://github.com/kshitijk4poor/hermes-rex-plugin
  commit ee19fe921d8dd0c2f1cec14d8cb080a4126b0754
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 kshitijk4poor (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's Rex integration as a Nodecode cell. What it keeps:
the `rex` CLI as the only outside thing it runs, with `--autostart=false` so a
call never starts a Rex server, a three-second deadline per call, and the pane
named by REX_SESSION and REX_BLOCK; the pane-opening argv the plugin's README
documents -- `run -b <pane> --split=… --ratio 35 --focus=false --label <name>
--keep-open -- <command>` -- so a long command is visible beside the session
rather than hidden; reading a pane back with `capture -b <pane> --trim`; the
window label as the session's own words, slugged to a few lowercase words
joined by hyphens, with one status glyph in front -- the plugin's own set, ●
busy, ? waiting, ✓ done, ■ stopped, ✗ failed -- read back from the window each
time so a rename by anyone keeps the rest; and the rule that only the glyph is
the integration's, never the label. The plugin's session-start text and skill
become the cell's manual and its tools' descriptions: where this session is,
that long-lived commands belong in a pane, that panes opened by a session are
read before they are closed, and that a pane never takes focus.

What is left out, and why. Hermes' event hooks -- pre_llm_call,
on_human_input_request, on_session_end, subagent_start and the kanban
dispatcher -- have no counterpart here, so there is no automatic ● while a turn
runs and no ? when the session waits: Nodecode's hook points are the tool call,
the round and the frame, none of which is a turn's start or end, and a glyph
written from every frame would be a subprocess per frame. The mark is set where
a session says it is: rex_label, /rex mark, and the four verbs. The macOS
alert (a notification through osascript when the pane is not in front) is left
out with the front-app test it needs, since a Nodecode shell already shows what
waits, and the plugin's pane-following Reader -- a Python script tailing a
transcript or a kanban log in a new pane -- has no transcript file to follow
here; a pane runs the command the model gives it. Rex's own session-start text
(`rex __internal session-start`) and its skill stub (`rex __internal skill`)
are likewise dropped: the cell's manual is written here, so nothing at run time
depends on which Rex version is installed, and nothing about another agent's
setup can contradict it. Automatic restoration of the window label a session
found is dropped too: the operator clears a mark with /rex mark clear.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell runs the same rex CLI the
plugin ran, and its own code is Common Lisp.
